# Meditations, Book I — modern-English candidate v1 (frozen text).
# One entry per numbered meditation, aligned 1:1 with Long's I.1–I.17.
BOOK = 1
CANDIDATE = [
# 1
"1. From my grandfather Verus I learned good morals and the governing of my temper.",
# 2
"2. From the reputation and memory of my father: modesty and a manly character.",
# 3
"3. From my mother: piety and generosity, and to abstain not only from evil deeds but even from evil thoughts; and further, simplicity in my way of living, far removed from the habits of the rich.",
# 4
"4. From my great-grandfather: not to have gone to the public schools, and to have had good teachers at home, and to know that on such things a man should spend freely.",
# 5
"5. From my tutor: to belong to neither the Green nor the Blue party at the races in the Circus, nor to be a partisan of the Parmularius or the Scutarius at the gladiators' fights; and from him too I learned to endure hard work, to want little, to work with my own hands, not to meddle in other people's affairs, and not to be quick to listen to slander.",
# 6
"6. From Diognetus: not to busy myself with trivial things; not to believe what miracle-workers and conjurers say about incantations and the driving out of spirits and such things; not to breed quails for fighting, nor to give myself up passionately to such things; to put up with frank speech; to have become close to philosophy, and to have heard first Bacchius, then Tandasis and Marcianus; to have written dialogues in my youth; and to have wanted a plank bed and a skin to lie on, and whatever else of that kind belongs to the Greek discipline.",
# 7
"7. From Rusticus I got the impression that my character needed improvement and discipline; and from him I learned not to be led astray into sophistic ambition, nor into writing on speculative matters, nor into delivering little moralizing speeches, nor into showing myself off as a man of rigorous discipline, or as one who does good deeds for display; and to keep away from rhetoric, and poetry, and fine writing; and not to walk about the house in my outdoor clothes, nor do other things of that kind; and to write my letters simply, like the letter Rusticus wrote from Sinuessa to my mother; and toward those who have offended me with words or done me wrong, to be easily appeased and reconciled as soon as they show a readiness to be reconciled; and to read carefully, and not to be satisfied with a superficial understanding of a book; and not to give my assent hastily to people who talk too much; and I owe it to him that I came to know the discourses of Epictetus, which he shared with me from his own collection.",
# 8
"8. From Apollonius I learned freedom of will and unwavering steadiness of purpose; to look to nothing else, not even for a moment, except reason; to be always the same, in sharp pains, at the loss of a child, and in long illness; to see clearly, in a living example, that the same man can be both most resolute and yielding, and not peevish in giving instruction; and to have had before my eyes a man who clearly considered his experience and his skill in expounding philosophical principles the least of his merits; and from him I learned how to accept from friends what are counted as favors, without being either humbled by them or letting them pass unnoticed.",
# 9
"9. From Sextus: a kindly disposition, and the example of a household governed in a fatherly way, and the idea of living according to nature; seriousness without affectation, and careful attention to the interests of friends, and tolerance of ignorant people and of those who form opinions without thought. He had the gift of adapting himself readily to everyone, so that his company was more agreeable than any flattery, and at the same time he was held in the highest veneration by those who kept company with him. He had the ability both to discover and to set in order, intelligently and methodically, the principles necessary for life; he never showed anger or any other passion, but was entirely free from passion and at the same time most affectionate; and he could express approval without noisy display, and he possessed much knowledge without ostentation.",
# 10
"10. From Alexander the grammarian: to refrain from fault-finding, and not to chide reproachfully those who used a barbarous or ungrammatical or strange-sounding expression, but to introduce deftly the very expression that ought to have been used, in the course of an answer, or in confirming what was said, or in joining an inquiry into the thing itself rather than the word, or by some other apt suggestion.",
# 11
"11. From Fronto I learned to observe what envy, duplicity and hypocrisy are in a tyrant, and that, in general, those among us who are called Patricians are rather lacking in fatherly affection.",
# 12
"12. From Alexander the Platonist: not often, nor without necessity, to say to anyone, or to write in a letter, that I have no time; nor to keep excusing the neglect of the duties we owe to those we live with by pleading urgent business.",
# 13
"13. From Catulus: not to be indifferent when a friend finds fault, even if he finds fault without reason, but to try to restore him to his usual disposition; to be ready to speak well of teachers, as is reported of Domitius and Athenodotus; and to love my children truly.",
# 14
"14. From my brother Severus: to love my kin, and to love truth, and to love justice; and through him I came to know Thrasea, Helvidius, Cato, Dion, Brutus; and from him I received the idea of a commonwealth with the same law for all, governed with regard to equal rights and equal freedom of speech, and the idea of a kingly rule that respects above all the freedom of the governed. I learned from him also consistency and unwavering steadiness in my regard for philosophy; and a disposition to do good, and to give to others readily, and to cherish good hopes, and to believe that I am loved by my friends; and in him I saw no concealment of his opinions about those he condemned, and that his friends never had to guess what he wished or did not wish, for it was quite plain.",
# 15
"15. From Maximus I learned self-mastery, and not to be turned aside by anything; and cheerfulness in all circumstances, illness included; and a just blend of sweetness and dignity in character; and to do what was set before me without complaining. I observed that everyone believed he thought as he spoke, and that in all he did he never had any bad intention; and he never showed astonishment or surprise, was never in a hurry, never put off doing a thing, was never perplexed or dejected, never laughed to cover his annoyance, and, on the other hand, was never passionate or suspicious. He was in the habit of doing kindnesses, was ready to forgive, and was free from all falsehood; and he gave the impression of a man who could not be turned from the right, rather than of a man who had been improved. I observed, too, that no man could ever think he was despised by Maximus, or ever venture to think himself the better man. He also had the art of being humorous in an agreeable way.",
# 16
"16. In my father I observed mildness of temper, and unchangeable resolve in the things he had decided after due deliberation; and no vanity about the things men call honors; and a love of work and perseverance; and a readiness to listen to anyone with something to propose for the common good; and unwavering firmness in giving to every man what he deserved; and a knowledge, gained from experience, of when to act vigorously and when to relax. And I observed that he had overcome all passion for boys; and that he considered himself no more than any other citizen; and that he released his friends from any obligation to dine with him or to attend him when he went abroad, and that those who had been kept from accompanying him by urgent circumstances always found him the same. I observed, too, his habit of careful inquiry in every matter of deliberation, and his persistence, and that he never stopped investigating because he was satisfied with the appearances that first presented themselves; and that his way was to keep his friends, neither tiring of them soon nor being extravagant in his affection; and to be content on all occasions, and cheerful; and to foresee things a long way off, and to provide for the smallest matter without display; and to check at once popular applause and all flattery; and to be ever watchful over what the administration of the empire required, and to be a good manager of its expenditure, and to bear patiently the blame he got for such conduct; and he was neither superstitious about the gods, nor did he court men with gifts, or by trying to please them, or by flattering the populace; but he showed sobriety in all things, and firmness, and never a mean thought or action, nor any love of novelty. And the things that in any way make life comfortable, of which fortune gives an abundant supply, he used without arrogance and without apology, so that when he had them he enjoyed them without affectation, and when he did not have them he did not miss them. No one could ever say of him that he was either a sophist or a flippant home-bred slave or a pedant; but everyone acknowledged him to be a man mature, complete, above flattery, and able to manage his own affairs and other men's. Besides this, he honored those who were true philosophers, and he did not reproach those who pretended to be philosophers, nor yet was he easily led by them. He was also easy in conversation, and he made himself agreeable without any offensive affectation. He took reasonable care of his body's health, not as a man greatly attached to life, nor out of concern for his appearance, nor yet carelessly, but so that by his own attention he very seldom needed the physician's art, or medicine, or external treatments. He was most ready to give, without envy, to those who had some particular ability, such as eloquence, or knowledge of the law, or of morals, or of anything else; and he gave them his help, so that each might enjoy the reputation he deserved; and he always acted in accordance with the institutions of his country, without any show of doing so. Further, he was not fond of change nor unsteady, but he liked to stay in the same places and to occupy himself with the same things; and after his attacks of headache he came back at once, fresh and vigorous, to his usual occupations. His secrets were not many, but very few and very rare, and those only about public matters; and he showed prudence and economy in putting on the public spectacles, and in the construction of public buildings, his gifts to the people, and such things, for he was a man who looked to what ought to be done, not to the reputation to be got from a man's acts. He did not bathe at unseasonable hours; he was not fond of building houses, nor fussy about what he ate, nor about the weave and color of his clothes, nor about the beauty of his slaves. His clothes came from Lorium, his villa on the coast, and generally from Lanuvium. We know how he behaved to the toll-collector at Tusculum who asked his pardon; and such was all his behavior. There was in him nothing harsh, nor implacable, nor violent, nor, as one might say, anything carried to the sweating point; but he examined all things one by one, as if he had plenty of time, and without confusion, in an orderly way, vigorously and consistently. And what is recorded of Socrates might be applied to him: that he was able both to abstain from, and to enjoy, those things which many are too weak to abstain from and cannot enjoy without excess. But to be strong enough both to bear the one and to be sober in the other is the mark of a man who has a perfect and invincible soul, such as he showed in the illness of Maximus.",
# 17
"17. To the gods I owe it that I had good grandfathers, good parents, a good sister, good teachers, good companions, good kinsmen and friends, nearly everything good. Further, I owe it to the gods that I was not hurried into any offense against any of them, though I had a disposition which, had the opportunity offered, might have led me to do something of the kind; but through their favor there was never such a concurrence of circumstances as put me to the test. Further, I am thankful to the gods that I was not brought up any longer with my grandfather's concubine; and that I kept the flower of my youth, and did not prove my manhood before the proper time, but even put off the time; that I was placed under a ruler and father who was able to strip me of all pride, and to bring me to the knowledge that it is possible for a man to live in a palace without needing either guards, or embroidered clothes, or torches and statues and suchlike show; but that it is in such a man's power to bring himself very near to the way of a private person, without being on that account either meaner in thought, or slacker in action, about the things that must be done for the common good in a manner that befits a ruler. I thank the gods for giving me such a brother, who by his moral character was able to rouse me to watchfulness over myself, and who at the same time pleased me by his respect and affection; that my children have been neither stupid nor deformed in body; that I did not get further in rhetoric, poetry and the other studies, in which I might perhaps have been completely absorbed if I had seen that I was making progress in them; that I made haste to set those who brought me up in the position of honor they seemed to want, without putting them off with the hope that I would do it some other time, because they were then still young; that I knew Apollonius, Rusticus, Maximus; that I received clear and frequent impressions of living according to nature, and of what kind of life that is, so that, as far as depended on the gods, and their gifts, and help, and inspirations, nothing prevented me from living according to nature at once, though I still fall short of it through my own fault, and through not heeding the admonitions of the gods and, I might almost say, their direct instructions; that my body has held out so long in such a kind of life; that I never touched either Benedicta or Theodotus, and that, after having fallen into passions of love, I was cured; and that, though I was often out of humor with Rusticus, I never did anything I had cause to regret; that, though it was my mother's fate to die young, she spent the last years of her life with me; that whenever I wished to help anyone in need, or on any other occasion, I was never told that I lacked the means to do it; and that I myself never came under the same necessity of receiving anything from another; that I have such a wife, so obedient, and so affectionate, and so simple; that I had plenty of good teachers for my children; and that remedies were shown to me in dreams, both others, and against blood-spitting and dizziness…; and that, when I took an inclination to philosophy, I did not fall into the hands of any sophist, and that I did not waste my time on writers of histories, or on the solving of syllogisms, or busy myself with the investigation of appearances in the heavens; for all these things require the help of the gods and of fortune. Written among the Quadi, at the Granua.",
]
